<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Please evaluate and advise on nicotine dependence, other tobacco product, uncomplicated and dysphagia, oropharyngeal phase and abnormal weight loss. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgent otolaryngology–head and neck surgery referral for a persistent right tonsillar lesion with painful swallowing in a heavy cigar smoker, for laryngoscopy and biopsy within two weeks. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Seen in otolaryngology–head and neck surgery consultation at the request of Corinne Batiste, MD. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Right-sided throat pain for four weeks, worse with swallowing, that has not improved. [S]</item><item>Wants a clear answer about the diagnosis quickly so he can plan for his family and finish his memoir. [S]</item><item>Has smoked cigars most of his life and is unsure he can stop. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Hiram Ulysses Grant was born on April 27, 1822, in Point Pleasant, Ohio, the first child of Jesse and Hannah Grant. [D]</item><item>Grant was a heavy cigar smoker: his tobacco use rose during the Civil War to about eighteen to twenty cigars a day, and he reported smoking nine or ten a day in peacetime. [D]</item><item>In June 1884, at his summer home in Long Branch, New Jersey, Grant began to have stinging throat pain and pain on swallowing. [D]</item><item>Grant had recurrent severe, incapacitating headaches, interpreted retrospectively as most likely migraines. [R]</item><item>In 1858 Grant had fever and chills, possibly from malaria or typhoid, and he had periodic fevers believed to have been malaria. [R]</item><item>Grant&apos;s father, Jesse Grant, died in 1873 at age 79; his mother, Hannah Grant, died in 1883. [D]</item></list><paragraph>7 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

